// specs.memql
//
// forge-namespace specs. Under the spec/shape binding redesign (epic
// #2281) a caller / role predicate is a `spec` bound to an @actor shape
// in its signature (it reads the projected envelope key by bare name and
// evaluates against the auth context). These gate the developer/owner-
// only read paths (the validation + approval queues) at the query layer,
// so non-developers literally cannot list other people's requests to act
// on them. Submission and "my own requests" stay open to every
// authenticated team member.
//
// Cluster roles are owner/admin/developer/writer/reader
// (dsl/identity/concepts.memql) -- there IS a literal "developer" role, and
// this comment used to deny it while the spec below silently excluded it
// (memql#4112). The forge personas map on:
//   owner     -> approver / fast-track
//   admin     -> senior developer (validate + approve)
//   developer -> senior developer (engineering power; validate)
//   writer    -> junior developer (validate)
//   reader    -> non-developer employee (submit-only, mentored)
//
// Keep forgeDeveloper in step with dsl/forge/tools.memql's @allowedRoles and
// with forgeRequestRoleAllowed (component/memql/forge_request_validation.go).
// When they disagree, the caller does NOT get an error: the MCP tool gate
// admits the call and the query's forgeDeveloper conjunct filters every row,
// so a legitimately-permissioned role reads it as "the queue is empty".

use common.shapes.{ actorEnvelope }

/// Caller is a forge developer (owner/admin/developer/writer) -- may see the validation queue and
/// validate requests.
spec actorEnvelope forgeDeveloper {
  return role == "owner" || role == "admin" || role == "developer" || role == "writer"
}

/// Caller is a forge approver (owner) -- holds final approval + fast-track authority.
spec actorEnvelope forgeApprover {
  return role == "owner"
}
